CAT = "ffiPpe"; SEG = "PPE"
Q = [
("The three layers of a structural firefighting coat are the outer shell, the thermal barrier and the:",
 "Moisture barrier", ["Reflective liner", "Kevlar sleeve", "Wool lining"],
 "Turnout coats and pants have an outer shell, a moisture barrier and a thermal barrier. Each layer must be in place for the garment to give its rated protection."),
("A PASS device sounds its full alarm when the wearer has not moved for about:",
 "30 seconds", ["10 seconds", "60 seconds", "2 minutes"],
 "A PASS (personal alert safety system) gives a pre-alarm and then a loud full alarm when the wearer stays motionless for about 30 seconds. It can also be set off manually."),
("Under the current NFPA SCBA standard, the low-air alarm sounds when about how much of the cylinder's air remains?",
 "About one-third", ["About one-half", "About one-quarter", "About one-tenth"],
 "The alarm was raised from one-quarter to about one-third of the air (33 percent of rated pressure since 2013; the 2025 NFPA 1970 sets it by air volume, 31 percent on a 4,500 psi cylinder). It means leave the hazard area now, not finish the task."),
("A cylinder rated for 30 minutes, used by a firefighter doing heavy work, will usually last:",
 "Less than 30 minutes", ["Exactly 30 minutes", "More than 30 minutes", "Until the PASS sounds"],
 "Rated duration is based on a standard breathing rate. Heavy work, stress and poor fitness use air much faster, so real working time is usually shorter."),
("An open-circuit SCBA is the type used in structural firefighting because:",
 "Exhaled air vents outside", ["It recycles exhaled air", "It needs no cylinder", "It filters smoke"],
 "Open-circuit SCBA supplies air from a cylinder and vents the exhaled air outside the facepiece. Closed-circuit units rebreathe scrubbed air and are used for long-duration work."),
("Air-purifying respirators are not used for interior firefighting because they:",
 "Cannot supply oxygen", ["Are too heavy to carry", "Have no facepiece", "Need a tank refill"],
 "An air-purifying respirator only filters the surrounding air. A fire atmosphere may lack oxygen and holds unknown contaminants, so SCBA is required."),
("Before each use of an SCBA facepiece, a firefighter checks the seal by:",
 "Doing a negative-pressure test", ["Shaking the cylinder", "Blowing into the regulator", "Loosening the whole head harness"],
 "Cover the regulator opening or connection and inhale; the facepiece should pull in and hold. A leak means readjust or replace before entering."),
("Facial hair that passes between the face and the sealing surface of an SCBA facepiece is:",
 "Not permitted", ["Fine if trimmed short", "Fine with a strap", "Allowed for officers"],
 "OSHA's respiratory rule forbids facial hair that interferes with the facepiece seal, because even a little hair lets contaminants in. Fit testing is done at least yearly."),
("A firefighter's SCBA facepiece fit test must be repeated at least:",
 "Once a year", ["Once a month", "Every five years", "Only when hired"],
 "Fit testing is required before first use and at least annually, and again if the face shape changes. Daily checks do not replace the fit test."),
("The SCBA cylinder pressure gauge and the remote gauge should read:",
 "Within about 100 psi", ["Within about 500 psi", "At least 300 psi apart", "Only the remote matters"],
 "Gauges should agree within the manufacturer's tolerance, commonly about 100 psi. A larger difference means the unit needs service before use."),
("Which part of the SCBA reduces cylinder pressure to a usable level?",
 "The regulator", ["The PASS device", "The backplate", "The harness"],
 "High-pressure air goes through a pressure-reducing regulator, then the facepiece regulator delivers it on demand. The PASS device and harness do not handle air."),
("A composite SCBA cylinder must be hydrostatically tested every:",
 "5 years", ["Year", "10 years", "15 years"],
 "U.S. DOT rules require hydrostatic testing of composite SCBA cylinders every five years. Most composite cylinders have a service life of 15 years."),
("When an SCBA cylinder is being filled, it should be placed:",
 "In a shielded fill station", ["On the tailboard", "In the firefighter's lap", "Under running water"],
 "A shielded fill station protects the operator if the cylinder ruptures. Fill slowly to limit heat buildup, which makes the gauge read high."),
("While wearing SCBA, a firefighter conserves air best by:",
 "Controlled breathing", ["Rapid shallow breaths", "Holding the breath", "Removing the facepiece"],
 "Controlled breathing, inhaling through the nose and exhaling slowly through the mouth, stretches air supply. Holding the breath raises carbon dioxide and stress, and removing the facepiece exposes the lungs to smoke."),
("Which is an emergency procedure for an SCBA regulator that fails during interior work?",
 "Open the bypass valve", ["Remove the facepiece", "Shut off the cylinder", "Wait for the PASS alarm"],
 "Opening the bypass or emergency valve sends air around the failed regulator; close it after each breath to save air, and leave the hazard area. Then call for help."),
("Reducing the profile of an SCBA to fit through a tight opening means:",
 "Loosening a strap and shifting the pack", ["Removing the facepiece first", "Shutting off the air supply", "Taking off the SCBA and pushing it ahead"],
 "A firefighter can loosen a shoulder strap and slide the cylinder to one side, keeping the facepiece on. Full removal is a last resort, and the hand never leaves the unit."),
("The hood worn under a helmet protects the:",
 "Ears and neck", ["Eyes and nose", "Hands and wrists", "Feet and ankles"],
 "The protective hood covers ears, neck and face areas not covered by the helmet and facepiece. Newer particulate-blocking hoods also reduce soot reaching the skin."),
("Helmet eye protection such as a faceshield must be:",
 "Backed by goggles or the facepiece", ["Used alone as primary eye protection", "Removed during interior work", "Worn only at vehicle fires"],
 "A helmet faceshield alone is not primary eye protection. Goggles or the SCBA facepiece give the actual protection."),
("Protective clothing should be inspected for damage:",
 "After each use", ["Only when issued", "Every five years", "Only after a fire"],
 "Members inspect their gear after each use and routinely, looking for rips, burns, contamination and damaged reflective trim. Advanced inspections are done at least yearly."),
("Turnout gear that is contaminated with soot should be:",
 "Washed per the maker's rules", ["Hung up in the bunk room to dry", "Washed at home", "Left to air out"],
 "Contaminated gear is cleaned in a gear extractor following the manufacturer's directions. Washing at home or keeping it in living areas spreads carcinogens."),
("Sweating inside turnout gear creates which hazard?",
 "Steam burns", ["Electric shock", "Frostbite", "Hearing loss"],
 "Water and sweat in the layers can turn to steam under heat and burn the skin. Wet gear also conducts heat faster."),
("Proximity suits with an aluminized outer shell are designed mainly for:",
 "High radiant heat", ["Chemical splashes", "Structural interiors", "Water rescue"],
 "Aluminized proximity gear reflects radiant heat at fires such as aircraft and flammable liquid fires. It is not chemical protection."),
("Wildland firefighting clothing differs from structural turnout gear by being:",
 "Lighter and flame resistant", ["Heavier and waterproof", "Fully gas tight", "Rated for interior structural fires"],
 "Wildland clothing is light flame-resistant fabric to limit heat stress during long, hard work. It does not protect against structural interior fire."),
("Which agency's rule requires SCBA in atmospheres immediately dangerous to life or health?",
 "OSHA", ["FAA", "EPA", "DOT"],
 "OSHA's respiratory protection standard requires SCBA for IDLH atmospheres and sets the two-in, two-out rule. DOT regulates cylinder testing and transport."),
("Hearing protection is commonly needed when a firefighter:",
 "Rides in a loud cab", ["Reads a gauge", "Washes hose", "Writes a report"],
 "Sirens, air horns, saws and pumps can cause lasting hearing loss. Intercom headsets or ear protection are worn where noise is high."),
("Before an SCBA goes back into service after a fire, the firefighter should:",
 "Refill and clean it", ["Leave the cylinder empty", "Store the facepiece wet", "Skip the daily check"],
 "After use, replace or refill the cylinder, clean and disinfect the facepiece, and check every part so the unit is ready for the next call."),
("When a firefighter hears the SCBA low-air alarm inside a building, the right action is to:",
 "Exit with the crew now", ["Finish the current task", "Switch off the alarm", "Share air and stay"],
 "The low-air alarm means leave the hazard area with your crew right away, while there is air left for the trip out. The exit can take longer than the way in."),
("Station or work uniforms worn under turnout gear should be:",
 "Non-melting fibers", ["Polyester blends", "Nylon sportswear", "Any fabric"],
 "Natural or flame-resistant fibers will not melt onto the skin if heat gets through the gear. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester and nylon can melt."),
]
